p=list(d.paragraphs)
def replace(i,text):
    pp=p[i]; pp.clear(); pp.add_run(text)
def before(i,text,style='Body Text'):
    return p[i].insert_paragraph_before(text,style)
def fig_before(i,file,caption):
    pp=before(i,''); pp.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER; pp.paragraph_format.keep_with_next=True
    pp.add_run().add_picture(str(ROOT/'figures'/file),width=Inches(6.2))
    cp=before(i,caption);cp.paragraph_format.space_after=Pt(8);cp.runs[0].font.size=Pt(9);cp.runs[0].font.color.rgb=RGBColor.from_string('526674')
def table_before(i,headers,rows):
    t=d.add_table(rows=1,cols=len(headers));t.alignment=WD_TABLE_ALIGNMENT.CENTER
    for j,h in enumerate(headers):t.rows[0].cells[j].text=h
    for row in rows:
        cells=t.add_row().cells
        for j,v in enumerate(row):cells[j].text=str(v)
    for n,row in enumerate(t.rows):
        trPr=row._tr.get_or_add_trPr(); trPr.append(OxmlElement('w:cantSplit'))
        for c in row.cells:
            c.vertical_alignment=WD_CELL_VERTICAL_ALIGNMENT.CENTER
            for pp in c.paragraphs:
                pp.paragraph_format.space_after=Pt(3);pp.paragraph_format.space_before=Pt(3)
                for r in pp.runs:r.font.size=Pt(9);r.bold=n==0
            if n==0:
                sh=OxmlElement('w:shd');sh.set(qn('w:fill'),'DCE8F0');c._tc.get_or_add_tcPr().append(sh)
    p[i]._p.addprevious(t._tbl)
    return t
replace(2,'Randall S. Young | Independent Researcher, Indiana, USA\nORCID: https://orcid.org/0009-0003-1657-0243\n23 November 2026 | Version 3.0 — preprint candidate; not peer reviewed')
replace(6,'Two computational benchmarks examine the trade-off: 30 seeded portfolios of matched combinatorial networks and a PDP-8 instruction-level multiplication benchmark. At 32 synthetic outputs, reuse reduces distinct nodes by 50.9% but increases mean motif-fault impact from 0.96 to 4.08 outputs; targeted buffering reduces the latter to 0.10, or 0.485 when output-integrator faults are included. At 64 PDP-8 tasks, a shared subroutine saves 70.8% of code words at a 4.1% modeled memory-cycle cost, but increases mean non-timeout routine-fault impact from 0.93 to 55.82 tasks. Dual modularity and redundant verification reduce this dependency. Wider fault injection reveals vulnerability in the coordination machinery itself. Exhaustive functional checks cover 16,384 multiplication results and 540 network comparisons across all 4,096 input states, without discrepancies.')
replace(7,'These results motivate a falsifiable coordination-constraint hypothesis: among comparable noncoding regulatory elements, weakly buffered higher-order downstream effects predict greater evolutionary constraint beyond conventional enhancer pleiotropy and target-gene identity. The PDP-8 serves as an experimental lens, not a mechanistic model of the cell. The computational findings establish a trade-off within specified models; the genomic prediction remains untested on real biological data. Evolutionary drift, historical contingency and context-specific costs preclude a claim of universal optimality.')
replace(12,'Our central thesis is that both biological organization and emerging technologies face pressure to achieve functional diversity with economical use of resources. Architecture—the organization, composition, reuse and coordination of components—mediates that objective. Reuse expands functionality without a proportional increase in machinery, but concentrates dependencies. Redundancy can protect these dependencies, yet its coordinating mechanisms can themselves become critical failure points. This nested trade-off, rather than simple minimization of components, is the organizing principle of this article.')
replace(21,'Compositional economy is illustrated by a Hilbert curve (Figure 1a). A compact recursive rule produces 4ⁿ − 1 segments at order n: 3, 15 and 255 at orders 1, 2 and 4. The figure demonstrates generative complexity without proportionally increasing the number of primitives. It is a mathematical illustration, not a claim that genomic development executes sequential recursion.')
fig_before(22,'fig1a_hilbert.png','Figure 1a. Emergent complexity from a compact recursive rule. Original schematic of orders 1, 2 and 4; the number of segments is 4ⁿ − 1. The curve illustrates composition rather than a literal mechanism of developmental regulation.')
replace(24,'Herbert Simon’s analysis of hierarchical, nearly decomposable systems [27] provides the theoretical bridge between the PDP-8 and the genome. Hierarchies make complex organization tractable when interactions within modules are stronger than interactions across modules. Kirschner and Gerhart’s conserved, versatile core processes [20] suggest how biological diversity arises through regulatory recombination. Kitano’s framework [21] emphasizes robustness trade-offs, and Castle et al. [22] connect evolvability to engineering design. Architectural economy integrates these perspectives: economy is a property of an arrangement of dependencies, not simply a count of parts.')
replace(26,'Compare architectures that realize a matched functional portfolio using a cost vector (static resources, dynamic operation, and reliability loss), rather than a single universal currency. An architecture Pareto-dominates another when it is no worse on every chosen metric and strictly better on at least one. A reused component increases the number of outputs that may be exposed to one fault; the magnitude depends on the actual function, context, redundancy and fault distribution. The proposed biological counterpart concerns fitness-relevant perturbations, not the assumption that all mutations are loss-of-function events.')
replace(50,'Comparative genomics demonstrates substantial purifying selection outside protein-coding exons. Bejerano et al. identified 481 ultraconserved elements (at least 200 bp identical across human, mouse and rat), many noncoding [18]. The Zoonomia 240-mammal analysis estimated that at least 332 million human bases (approximately 10.7%) are unusually conserved and identified 4,552 nearly perfectly conserved elements [4]. Coding sequence is generally more constrained per nucleotide; the large quantity of noncoding sequence means many constrained bases are noncoding. Deleting selected ultraconserved enhancers near Arx produces viable mice with neurological and developmental abnormalities [19], establishing contributions to normal development for those elements, not universal essentiality.')
replace(56,'H1 (architectural economy): Reuse of composable operations can reduce static implementation cost relative to functionally equivalent dedicated designs, while increasing shared fault exposure. Modularity and targeted redundancy can reduce exposure; the resulting architecture must be evaluated against the additional cost and vulnerability of its coordinating machinery.')
replace(57,'H2 (coordination-constraint): Within a defined class of functional noncoding elements, broad, weakly buffered higher-order downstream coordination predicts greater sequence constraint after controlling for sequence context, enhancer activity breadth, direct target count and invariant target-gene properties. This is a prediction about associations under a specified empirical design, not evidence that all noncoding DNA is functional.')
replace(62,'Two Python benchmarks examine fan-out (regulatory-like reuse) and fan-in (subroutine-like reuse). The v2 implementation and archived JSON data are retained; a separate audit program in audit/reproduce_audit.py extends functional testing, measures executable capacity, and tests other tracked instructions. All numerical results are conditional on the stated workloads, architectures and fault models.')
replace(64,'Matched portfolios contain K = 4, 8, 12, 16, 24 or 32 outputs over 12 binary inputs; 30 seeded portfolios per K were evaluated on 256 sampled states. Each output integrates two three-input motifs sampled from a Zipf-distributed library of 16 primitives. Four architectures implement the same portfolio: dedicated private motifs; fully shared motifs; modular sharing with fan-out capped at four; and modular sharing with disjunctive shadow copies for motifs serving at least two outputs. Activation cost counts active modeled internal nodes, not biological energy or gate-level circuit cost. The loss-of-function model forces a motif or output-integrator node to zero; blast radius counts outputs changed on at least one test input.')
replace(65,'Table 2. Synthetic networks at K = 32 (mean ± SD across 30 seeds). Fault metrics here refer to motif-node knockouts only. All-node measures are reported separately below.')
replace(66,'At K = 32, full sharing reduces nodes by 50.9%, edges by 57.3% and modeled activation cost by 46.9%, but raises mean motif-fault impact 4.3-fold. The mean across seeds of maximum motif-fault impact rises from 1.00 to 13.63 outputs; bounded fan-out limits it to 4.00. Buffered modular networks require 24.6% fewer nodes and 22.6% lower activation cost than the dedicated baseline, and have a lower mean motif-fault blast radius (0.10 versus 0.96). The maximum is tied at 1.00; buffering therefore Pareto-dominates on the reported aggregate metrics, rather than strictly improving every metric. A broader fault model including output integrators yields the more modest, but still favorable, comparison in Table 2a.')
replace(68,'Figure 7. Controlled synthetic network comparison (30 seeds; K = 4–32). Panel C shows the cost–vulnerability plane at K = 32. Only modeled motif faults are represented; the shaded region denotes improvement over the dedicated baseline on the plotted metrics.')
replace(69,'Benchmark B: 12-bit DEC PDP-8 instruction-level model (fan-in regime)')
replace(70,'The PDP-8 model implements the memory-reference instructions and OPR subset needed for the benchmark, 4,096-word core memory, page-zero/current-page addressing, indirect addressing and auto-index registers. IOT is a placeholder NOP; interrupts, peripheral timing and other processor features are not fully modeled. The model counts memory cycles for its implemented instruction subset; no claim of complete hardware or cycle fidelity is made. Six-bit unsigned shift-and-add multiplication workloads contain N = 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 or 64 tasks. Four implementations are compared: inlined, single JMS subroutine, dual modular subroutines, and redundant comparison/fallback (two primary routines; a third on mismatch). One routine word at a time is replaced with NOP. Non-timeout faults and all routine faults are reported separately; a timeout is an operational failure, not proof of an infinite loop.')
replace(71,'Table 3. PDP-8 benchmark at N = 64. Code-space estimates are theoretical and do not demonstrate executable task capacity. All-fault columns refer to the enumerated routine words, not every program or machine fault.')
replace(72,'Shared JMS subroutines reduce the code footprint by 70.8% (1,631 to 477 words) at +4.1% modeled memory cycles (8,868 to 9,228). Mean non-timeout routine-fault blast radius increases from 0.93 to 55.82 tasks. Dual modularity adds 24 code words and reduces this value to 28.94. Redundant comparison/fallback reduces it to 2.25 and masks 96.5% of non-timeout routine faults, at 2.14 times the shared implementation’s modeled cycles (2.225 times inlining). Across all routine faults, including timeouts, the redundant implementation averages 8.13 disrupted tasks versus 5.91 for inlining. Thus redundancy improves one conditional fault metric but is not universally more robust. The serial program counter and shared wrapper create failure dependencies distinct from a distributed biological network.')
replace(74,'Figure 8. PDP-8 instruction-level benchmark for N = 1–64 shift-and-add multiplication tasks. Panel C shows the trade-off at N = 64 among static code memory, modeled memory cycles and non-timeout routine-fault blast radius.')
